concern:
  heading: "Fabricated citations, and confidentiality"
  body: >
    <p>We start with the failure the profession has already read about. General-purpose AI
    tools invent case citations that look entirely real — correct format, plausible names,
    confident tone — and practitioners in several jurisdictions have been sanctioned for
    filing them. It is a predictable property of how the tools work, not a rare glitch, and
    training that glosses over it is dangerous. We teach it as a core module: never cite
    what you have not opened, treat every authority as unverified until checked in a real
    database, and understand why the output sounds certain either way.</p>
    <p>On confidentiality: client information is subject to privilege and to your conduct
    rules. The program covers which tools carry terms that make business use defensible,
    what must never be pasted into a consumer tier, and how to build workflows where
    privileged material stays inside systems you control. Your firm finishes with a written
    policy your principal signs off before anything runs on live matters.</p>
