faq:
  - q: "How is this different from a one-day AI workshop?"
    a: "A workshop teaches awareness; three months changes behaviour — and in this profession it also builds the verification habit, which one day cannot. Practitioners leave a workshop aware that AI fabricates citations and still have no working discipline for catching it. Our program runs across three months on your own matters, and the ongoing support keeps it current as the tools change."
  - q: "Isn't AI too risky for legal work?"
    a: "Untrained use is risky, and that risk is already inside your firm — staff are using these tools whether or not there's a policy. Trained use, with verification built in and the boundaries understood, is considerably safer than the unsupervised use happening now. Closing that gap is the entire argument for the program."
  - q: "What does it cost?"
    a: "It's quoted per team size, and the three months is a fixed cost rather than an open-ended engagement, so you know the total before you start. Tell us how many practitioners and support staff you'd send and we'll give you the number, along with the work we'd expect to compress first."
  - q: "Can it do legal research?"
    a: "Research belongs in a proper legal database, and we train people to keep it there — general-purpose models have no authoritative access to Australian case law and will fabricate confidently. AI earns its place once you have the material: summarising, structuring, drafting and explaining."
  - q: "Will this help support staff as well as solicitors?"
    a: "Often more. Legal secretaries, paralegals and practice managers do a large share of the drafting, formatting and correspondence, and that's the work that compresses most. Many firms start there and bring practitioners in after."
  - q: "We bill by the hour. What does this do to revenue?"
    a: "It converts unbillable drafting time into billable matter time, which is where most firms are constrained — the writing around the work was never the part clients were paying for. Firms on fixed-fee or capped work see it land straight as margin."
  - q: "Do we need a firm-wide AI policy first?"
    a: "Writing it is part of the program rather than a prerequisite. Teams draft their own during the course, informed by how the tools actually behave rather than by guesswork, and the principal signs it off before it applies to live matters."
